/* fox_crow.lps — Kowalski, ch. 3: the fox and the crow.
 *
 * examples/moreExamples/rkBook/fox_and_crow.le has the fox's *beliefs* — LE
 * can say that praising an animal makes it sing, and that a singing animal
 * drops what it is holding. What LE cannot say is the part that makes it a
 * story: the fox has a goal, it acts, time passes, and the world changes as a
 * result.
 *
 * §3.4 asks "the end of the story?" — the crow could learn. The last rule is
 * that, and it is a maintenance goal on the crow's side: once fooled, refuse
 * to sing. Two agents' goals in one program, which is what a cycle buys.
 */

maxTime(10).

fluents  has(_, _), near(_, _), sings(_), fooled(_).
events   sees(_, _).
actions  praise(_, _), pick_up(_, _), drop(_, _), sing(_).

initially has(crow, cheese), near(fox, crow).

praise(_, Who)  initiates sings(Who).
sing(Who)       initiates sings(Who).
drop(Who, What) terminates has(Who, What).
drop(Who, What) initiates near(ground, What).
pick_up(Who, W) initiates has(Who, W).
pick_up(_, W)   terminates near(ground, W).
sees(_, _)      initiates fooled(crow).

%  The fox's goal, and its plan: praise, wait for the song, take the cheese.
if   has(crow, cheese) at T1, near(fox, crow) at T1, not has(fox, cheese) at T1
then praise(fox, crow) from T1 to T2,
     sings(crow) at T3,
     pick_up(fox, cheese) from T3 to T4.

%  Singing with your mouth full is how the story works.
if   sings(Who) at T1, has(Who, What) at T1
then drop(Who, What) from T1 to T2.

%  §3.4: the crow, having been fooled once, stops singing when praised. A
%  constraint on the crow rather than a belief about it.
false sing(crow) from T1 to _, fooled(crow) at T1.

observe sees(crow, fox_taking_cheese) from 6 to 7.
